## Quick start

<% if (includeDocker) { -%>
### With Docker

```bash
npm run up
```

| Service  | URL |
| -------- | --- |
| Frontend | <%= feDockerUrl %> |
| Backend  | <%= beUrl %> |
| Mail (Mailpit) | http://localhost:8025 |
| Postgres | localhost:5432 (`postgres` / `postgres`, db `saas_app`) |

Nothing is emailed for real in this setup — every message the backend sends is caught by
Mailpit and read at the URL above.

<% } -%>
### Locally

You need a PostgreSQL 16 database first:

```bash
docker run --name saas-pg -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres -e POSTGRES_DB=saas_app -p 5432:5432 -d postgres:16-alpine
```

**Backend** — serves <%= beUrl %>

```bash
cd backend
cp .env.example .env
<% if (backend.language === 'python') { -%>
python -m venv .venv
# Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate | macOS/Linux: source .venv/bin/activate
<% } -%>
cd ..
npm run install:backend
npm run dev:backend
```

Edit `backend/.env` before starting. `DATABASE_URL` must match your database, and
`JWT_SECRET` must be at least 32 characters — the server refuses to start otherwise,
rather than signing real tokens with a placeholder. Generate one with:

```bash
openssl rand -base64 48
```

Tables are created automatically on first boot.

**Frontend** — serves <%= feDevUrl %>

```bash
cd frontend && cp .env.example .env && cd ..
npm run install:frontend
npm run dev:frontend
```

`<%= frontend.apiUrlEnv %>` in `frontend/.env` must point at the backend (<%= beUrl %>).

## Project scripts

Every task has a root script, so you never have to remember which stack is underneath:

| Command | What it does |
| ------- | ------------ |
| `npm run dev:backend` | Start the API in watch mode |
| `npm run dev:frontend` | Start the web app in watch mode |
| `npm run install:backend` | Install backend dependencies |
| `npm run install:frontend` | Install frontend dependencies |
| `npm run admin -- you@example.com` | Promote an account to admin |
| `npm test` | Run the API compliance suite |
<% if (includeDocker) { -%>
| `npm run up` / `npm run down` | Start / stop the whole stack in Docker |
| `npm run logs` | Tail the backend container logs |
| `npm run psql` | Open a psql shell on the database |
| `npm run admin:docker -- you@example.com` | Promote an account, against the running container |
<% } -%>

## Create your first admin user

There is no seed script and no default admin. Every registration gets `role = 'user'`, so
`/admin` stays inaccessible until you promote an account yourself:

1. Register through the frontend.
2. Promote it:

```bash
npm run admin -- you@example.com
<% if (includeDocker) { -%>
# or, if the stack is running in Docker:
npm run admin:docker -- you@example.com
<% } -%>
```

3. Log out and back in — the role is carried in the JWT, so an old token still says `user`.

## Testing against the contract

`tests/compliance/` is a backend-agnostic suite that checks this API against
[`docs/openapi.yaml`](docs/openapi.yaml): response shapes, status codes, `snake_case`
keys, auth enforcement, and the full email and 2FA flows. Run it after changing an
endpoint to confirm you did not break the contract.

```bash
npm test
```

It reads `tests/compliance/.env.defaults` (already pointed at <%= beUrl %>), so it works
with no arguments. Some checks skip themselves until you give them more:

- the **12 admin checks** need `ADMIN_EMAIL` and `ADMIN_PASSWORD`
- the **9 email/2FA flow checks** need a reachable `MAILPIT_URL`<%= includeDocker ? ' — `npm run up` starts one' : '' %>

Put credentials in `tests/compliance/.env` (git-ignored, overrides the defaults):

```
ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com
ADMIN_PASSWORD=your-password
```

## Sending email

Email verification, password reset, and email 2FA all need SMTP. Without `SMTP_*` set in
`backend/.env` those flows cannot complete.
<% if (includeDocker) { -%>
The Docker setup wires this up for you. For local development, run a mail catcher and
point the backend at it:
<% } else { -%>
For local development, run a mail catcher and point the backend at it:
<% } -%>

```bash
docker run -p 1025:1025 -p 8025:8025 axllent/mailpit
# then in backend/.env: SMTP_HOST=localhost, SMTP_PORT=1025
```

Alternatively turn verification off via `PUT /api/admin/settings/email-verification` with
`{"require_email_verification": false}`.

## Features

- **Authentication** — register, login, JWT access/refresh tokens, logout, logout-all
- **Two-Factor Auth** — email 2FA, TOTP (authenticator apps), recovery codes
- **Google OAuth** — sign in with Google
- **Password management** — forgot/reset password, email verification
- **User profile** — view and update
- **Admin console** — user CRUD, role management, audit log
- **Analytics** — registration trends, active users, retention, cohort heatmap
- **App settings** — runtime toggles for email verification and 2FA
- **Rate limiting** — per-IP throttling on `/auth`, with admin unblock
- **i18n** — 7 locales (en, tr, de, fr, es, it, ru)
